A||N||20||Will||In depth turbo question: compressor outlet temp||||1||
Z||000000||Will||12-27-2000||12:19 PM||||What would the temp at the outlet of a centrifugal compressor pushing 15-20 psi of boost be? Is there a way to figure this out from the specs of the turbo in question? How much does this vary with compressor size? Most (all?) turbos use centrifugal compressors, right?<P>In the end what I'm trying to figure out is how much heat an intercooler would have to deal with for a heavily boosted engine.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sillhouette.com||12-27-2000||01:12 PM||||There are a lot of variables that come into play.  First the most heat will happen at the highest pressure (20 psi) and at the highest turbo speed (WOT) add to that the exhaust temperature and that is why Turbos cook and you need a turbo timer to let it cool down before shut down.  An intercooler is a great idea just wondering where are you going to install it on a fiero since it's a rear engine and not a lot of air circulates throught the engine unless you use some sort of ducting system.  What I have seen is powder coated manifolds and exhaust to reduce that temperature.  Also seen a water cooled intercooler using engine coolant.  Reliability is an issue with those ones because of the multitude of fittings and hoses that might break. <BR>Do your homework in selecting a turbo.  Not too big and not too small.  Too big of a turbo will give your better temperature handling properties but large turbo lag. Too small turbo will give you lots of power minmal lag but will cook in short order.  <P>Hope this helps.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||12-27-2000||01:40 PM||||...and I'll add to what Sillhouette.com said that picking an intercooler is just as important as the turbo itself. <P>Most people think that 'any' intercooler would be a benifit, but if its too small and not that efficent (lots has to do with placement also), then you'll get worse performance than no IC at all.  <P>If you just add an intercooler to an existing  blower/turbo set up with nothing else done (same boost/timing), then the gains you realized from a cooler air charged air won't justify the cost of one.  More boost must be dialed in to overcome the pressure loss you'll get with the IC to have the same manifold pressure as before, and then start bumping up your timing (most likely have a custom WOT timing curve for best results), then the benifits will start to outweigh your purchase of the IC.  Combo-combo-combo.<P>Sorry...went off on a tanget here....<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||12-27-2000||01:56 PM||||I am investigating the feasibility of using an air-freon intercooler, and need to know how much system capacity is necessary.<P>When using the ideal gas law, both the volumetric flow rate and the temperature after compression are unknown. I can't solve one equation with two unknowns.<P>I know 99% of what I need to know to solve this problem, but I still need to know what the compressor outlet temperature is! If anyone knows, don't be shy!<P>Silhouette:<BR>The high temp of a small turbo is solely a result of its low "thermal mass" compared to a large turbo.<BR>The process may be the same, but manifolds are not powder coated. When they are coated, they are usually coated with a ceramic. This coating should only be on the inside. Any kind of insulated coating on the outside of headers will increase the temperature of the metal enormously and shorten header life through the stresses of high temperature thermal cycling.<BR>     For this same reason, wrapping the outside of headers, manifolds, or exhaust pipes is not the best solution to engine compartment heat.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||12-27-2000||02:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BBBAD GT:<BR><B>...and I'll add to what Sillhouette.com said that picking an intercooler is just as important as the turbo itself. <P>Most people think that 'any' intercooler would be a benifit, but if its too small and not that efficent (lots has to do with placement also), then you'll get worse performance than no IC at all.  <P>If you just add an intercooler to an existing  blower/turbo set up with nothing else done (same boost/timing), then the gains you realized from a cooler air charged air won't justify the cost of one.  More boost must be dialed in to overcome the pressure loss you'll get with the IC to have the same manifold pressure as before, and then start bumping up your timing (most likely have a custom WOT timing curve for best results), then the benifits will start to outweigh your purchase of the IC.  Combo-combo-combo.<P>Sorry...went off on a tanget here....<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I am aware of the intricacies of playing with a boosted engine. I only need to know compresor outlet temperature so that I can figure out how much heat the intercooler will need to be able to remove from the intake charge.||||reg||1
Z||000005||gixxer||12-27-2000||04:26 PM||||You need to know two "absolute" pressures. One is ambient, and the other is the boost pressure you desire to run. One variable is compressor efficiency at that boost pressure. You need a compresser map for that specific turbo to see what it's efficiency is, or you could make an educated guess. 200 HP is about 300 CFM for the flow.<BR>There, you tapped ME out!<BR>Good luck.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||12-27-2000||05:12 PM||||Here's what I have so far:<P>PV=nRT (ideal gas law)<P>(P1V1)/(n1T1) = R = (P2V2)/(n2T2)<P>Where<BR>R is ideal gas constant (doesn't matter here)<BR>P1 is ambient pressure (~15 psi)<BR>V1 is volumetric flow rate at compressor inlet (34,000 litre/min ~ 1200 cfm; not necessarily one compressor)<BR>T1 is ambient temp (300K ~ 27 C ~ 80 F)<BR>n1 is mass flow rate at compressor inlet(40,000 g/min)<P>P2 is 35 psi<BR>V2 is volumetric flow rate at compressor outlet (not known)<BR>T2 is temperature at compressor outlet (also not known)<BR>n2 is mass flow rate at compressor outlet (same as n1)<P>I had heard 2 cfm per HP as a rule of thumb.<BR>Other numbers required:<BR>Density of air at 300K, 1 atm: 1.1765 g/l<BR>Specific heat of air at 300K, 1 atm: 1.007 J/(gK)<P>One problem is that I don't know what ballpark to drop my educated guess into.<BR>Even an estimate of compression ratio would let me solve the equation.<P>I've heard the term "compressor efficiency" before. How exactly is that defined? Is it something like the relationship of temperature change to pressure change?<BR>From the equation above, doubling the pressure (P2=2P1) could halve the volume while leaving temperature constant, double the temperature while leaving pressure constant, or anything in between.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||gixxer||12-27-2000||05:15 PM||||Here we are. <BR>In the book "Maximum Boost", I found a graph chart: pressure ratio vs compressor efficiency with the resultant compressor discharge temps. <BR>Let me know approx what boost levels you're considering, and I can find out what the temps would be with a variety of efficiencys.<BR>Good for a starter anyway....||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||12-27-2000||05:32 PM||||I'd like to estimate outlet temp for 15 and 20 psi.<P>Does the book say what kind of turbo the graph is for?<P>I'd like to use a water-cooled T3. I wonder if the turbo in your book is similar to a T3.<P>Anyway, thanks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||gixxer||12-27-2000||07:40 PM||||The chart in the book is generic. No compressor maps are shown for a T3 (AFR?) turbo, altho you might try the internet.<BR>Peak efficiency seems to be about 80% on a typical comp. map. <BR>Enough talk!<BR>Here's my rather rough pencil-and-ruler-on-my-lap findings.<BR>15 PSI (pressure ratio of 2.02)<BR>80%--237*F<BR>75%--245*F<BR>70%--257*F<BR>65%--268*F<BR>60%--280*F<BR>20 PSI (pressure ratio of 2.36)<BR>80%--275*F<BR>75%--287*F<BR>70%--305*F<BR>65%--320*F<BR>60%--340*F<BR>No effort was made to cross check this with any other method, and if I made a mistake, blame it on the LASIK surgeons in Canada!<BR>Hope this helps...<BR>   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||gixxer||12-27-2000||08:28 PM||||For a variety of T3 compressor maps see: <A HREF="http://www.proturbo.com/main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.proturbo.com/main.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||12-28-2000||09:08 AM||||Will:  Get back to us as to your findings with the A/C as intercooler idea.  Several people have tried it with supercharging a 5.0, but haven't heard much after the 'trying' stages....<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||12-29-2000||09:54 AM||||I was told on the forced induction section of the LS1.com forum that a 60 trim compressor with a 0.6 A/R housing would supply 15 psi at 30 lb/min, and that the discharge temp under these conditions would be 250 F. Assuming a worst case scenario of 300 F outlet temp, the intercooler would need a capacity of 3200 BTU/min to return the charge to ambient. This is at least 6 (six!) times the capacity of the stock Fiero A/C (assuming it's at most 30,000 BTU/hr--don't have my shop manual here right now). While not impossible, this does present some engineering difficulties.||||reg||1
Z||000013||bubbajoexx||12-31-2000||11:23 AM||||warning if you are going to use an inter cooler install a pyrometer in the exhaust outlet of the turbo as close to the outlet as possible because if the outlet temp reaches 1120 degrees farenheight by by motor you will melt down the valves in the head exhaust valves that is turbos are bad for that when inter cooled turbos need to get rid of the heat and an inter cooler will restict this coolingif not over sized.||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||12-31-2000||01:20 PM||||this is way to technical for me, but couldnt u just put like a baking thermometer in the turbo charger outlet tube and measure the temp of air passing thru it? ||||reg||1
Z||000015||BBBAD GT||12-31-2000||03:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bubbajoexx:<BR><B>warning if you are going to use an inter cooler install a pyrometer in the exhaust outlet of the turbo as close to the outlet as possible because if the outlet temp reaches 1120 degrees farenheight by by motor you will melt down the valves in the head exhaust valves that is turbos are bad for that when inter cooled turbos need to get rid of the heat and an inter cooler will restict this coolingif not over sized.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What?  I guess I don't understand what you are saying.  My EGT meter (1" from the head) on my 5.0 reads 1200 normally.  The stock valves can handle 1500 with no problems, and they aren't any type of 'severe duty' items.<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cooter||01-01-2001||05:37 PM||||I looked at the compressor maps but I am not a rocket scientist- could someone explain how to read them? I have the compressor map for that big turbo that I bought off eBay but it looks different than those on the proturbo website.<BR>As close as I can tell, the map that is closest to mine is the TSO4- how big is that? What size engine would it be suited for?<p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 01-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Will||01-01-2001||07:16 PM||||For the maps at proturbo.com, mass flow is along the horizontal axis and pressure ratio is on the vertical axis.<BR>For average BSFC of 0.5, 1 lb/min of air will combine with enough fuel to produce 10 HP.<BR>Pressure ratio is the ratio of the absolute pressures on the inlet and outlet sides of the compressor. Atmospheric pressure is about 15 psi, so 15 psi of boost would be an absolute pressure of 30 psi and a pressure ratio of 2.<BR>The point on the map described by your mass-flow and prssure ratio will tell you at what efficiency and RPM the compressor will be operating.||||reg||1
Z||000018||RedOktober||01-02-2001||09:34 PM||||Here Is a site for all you TURBO FREEKS!!!!!<P>It has a program that will tell you the size of turbo you need to run just by filling in some details..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.turbofast.com.au/TFmatch.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.turbofast.com.au/TFmatch.html</A> <P>He will even give you the trim and compressor sizes.  <P>Have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"||||reg||1
Z||000019||theogre||01-02-2001||09:45 PM||||If you are still thinking about that refrigerant using intercooler you should carefully check the legal side of it. Use of refrigerants in cars is very heavily regulated. Big fines and time if you get cought with something not legal. Especially anything in the Freon family.<P>On the practicl side. I doubt you could process that much heat in the space you have. The problem is the condenser capacity. There's only so much space for one and you're only alowed so much heat rise thru it or you won't be able to cool the radiator.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Will||01-03-2001||08:50 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>On the practicl side. I doubt you could process that much heat in the space you have. The problem is the condenser capacity. There's only so much space for one and you're only alowed so much heat rise thru it or you won't be able to cool the radiator.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Didn't I already say it was problematic (or words to that effect)?<BR>Using the rule of thumb that exhaust, coolant, and wheels each get 1/3 of an engine's energy, a 135 HP engine would need to dissipate more than 6000 BTU/min. Of course a larger more powerful engine would need to dissipate more. I don't think that 12,000 BTU/min of cooling system capacity would be unreasonable. While 3200 BTU/min isn't pocket change, neither is it an overwhelming heat load.<BR>I think that the cooling demands of both systems could be met with large radiator, large condensor, hood louvres  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> and ducting to ensure a positive flow throught he radiator  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Check out <A HREF="http://magi.com/~gaube/corvair/norris04.html" TARGET=_blank>http://magi.com/~gaube/corvair/norris04.html</A> <BR>This is a site about a mid-engined V8 Corvair and the setup the builder used for cooling air handling. The biggest conceptual difference is that the air through a Fiero radiator should go up through the hood and over the top of the car, rather than under the car.||||reg||1
